    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>For many renters, the “popcorn ceiling”, the bumpy, cottage-cheese-like texture known in the UK as an Artex or textured ceiling, is a persistent eyesore. It traps dust, creates harsh shadows and makes even the most stylish flat feel dated. The biggest challenge? As a renter, you can’t reach for a scraper. Not only is it messy, but older textured ceilings may contain asbestos, and disturbing them is a health hazard as well as a reliable way to lose your deposit.</p>
<p>Fortunately, you don’t have to live in a popcorn prison. This guide focuses on one goal: changing how the room looks while leaving the ceiling exactly as you found it, so you get your deposit back. If you want the wider picture of disguising the texture, read our companion guide on <a href="/blog/hide-popcorn-ceiling-renting">how to hide a popcorn ceiling while renting</a>. Here, we start with what protects you and your deposit, then work through the most popular cover-up ideas, including the ones we’d skip.</p>

<h2 id="safety-and-your-deposit">Safety first: asbestos, fire and your deposit</h2>
<h3 id="asbestos-check">Asbestos</h3>
<p>In the UK, textured ceiling coatings applied before 1999 may contain asbestos (US guidance often cites ceilings from before the 1980s). There is no way to tell by looking.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Treat the ceiling as hands-off.</strong> Don’t stick, tape, glue, pin, screw, drill, paint, scrape or sand it. Anything that touches the surface can loosen texture and, on an older ceiling, release fibres.</li>
<li><strong>Ask your landlord or letting agent, in writing,</strong> whether the ceiling has been tested. Testing and any work on the ceiling are their responsibility and should be done by qualified professionals.</li>
<li><strong>If the texture is cracked, flaking or water-damaged,</strong> report it in writing and leave it alone.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="fire-check">Fire safety</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use <strong>inherently flame-retardant fabric</strong> for anything hung overhead, and check the label.</li>
<li>Keep fabric well clear of light fittings, bulbs, heaters, smoke alarms and sprinkler heads. Never cover or obstruct an alarm.</li>
<li>Check your tenancy agreement and any building fire rules, particularly in flats.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="deposit-check">Protecting your deposit</h3>
<p>Your check-in inventory is your best defence. If the ceiling isn’t clearly recorded, take your own dated photos and send them to your agent. Get written permission for anything fixed to walls, and test any removable adhesive product on a hidden spot first.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Photograph every ceiling and the top 30 cm (12 in) of every wall on move-in day, in daylight, and email the photos to yourself and your letting agent so they carry a date.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="fabric-draping">1. Fabric draping</h2>
<p>Fabric draping is one of the most dramatic ways to soften a popcorn ceiling. It changes the architectural feel of a room, adding softness and a touch of bohemian luxury, and it can help dampen sound a little too.</p>
<p>Choose lightweight fabrics such as linen, muslin or thin cotton; heavier fabrics sag and strain their supports. The deposit-safe way to hang them is from the walls, not the ceiling:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wall-mounted hooks just below the ceiling line.</strong> Removable adhesive hooks on the walls can carry light swags. Check the weight rating against the fabric, test a hook on a hidden patch of wall first, and follow the maker’s removal instructions when you move out. On fragile or freshly painted walls, ask your landlord before using any adhesive.</li>
<li><strong>Tension rods wall to wall</strong> (see below), set below the ceiling, can hold flatter, more structured panels.</li>
<li><strong>A freestanding frame,</strong> such as a canopy bed or a freestanding canopy over a reading nook, needs no fixings at all.</li>
</ul>
<p>Let the fabric hang in soft swags with a clear gap above it so it never rests on the texture, and keep it away from the ceiling light and smoke alarm. This works especially well in bedrooms and reading corners where you want a cosy, enclosed feel.</p>

<h2 id="styrofoam-tiles">2. Styrofoam tiles: why we don’t recommend them</h2>
<p>Expanded polystyrene (EPS) ceiling tiles in faux tin or ornate plaster patterns are often promoted as a renter hack, usually fixed with tape, putty or a dab of glue. We don’t recommend them. Polystyrene tiles are a recognised fire hazard, and any method of sticking them up, even over a layer of tape, means adhering to a surface that may contain asbestos. Peeling them off at the end of a tenancy can pull texture away with them, which is exactly the damage your landlord will deduct for.</p>
<p>If you love the idea of a decorative ceiling, get the same overhead interest from a flame-retardant fabric canopy, or put pattern on a wall at eye level instead.</p>

<h2 id="strategic-lighting">3. Strategic lighting</h2>
<p>Sometimes the best way to hide something is to make sure no one notices it. Popcorn ceilings look worst when light hits them from close by or at a shallow angle, because every bump casts a tiny shadow. That’s why flush ceiling lights are the enemy of a textured ceiling.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep the light low.</strong> Use floor lamps that point downwards, table lamps with opaque or lined shades, and LED strips behind the TV or along the back of low furniture. With most of the light in the bottom two-thirds of the room, the ceiling sits in soft shadow.</li>
<li><strong>Move uplighters away from the walls,</strong> at least 60–90 cm (2–3 ft) into the room, so light meets the ceiling evenly rather than grazing it.</li>
<li><strong>Choose warm, diffused bulbs.</strong> Frosted or opal bulbs around 2700K are kinder to texture than bright cool-white or clear filament bulbs.</li>
<li><strong>Add a statement pendant without a ceiling hook.</strong> A plug-in swag pendant hung from a wall bracket, with the cable run neatly down the wall, gives you a downward-facing focal point that draws the eye to the light itself. Check the bracket’s weight rating and get permission for any wall fixing.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are allowed to change the existing ceiling fitting, that is a job for your landlord’s electrician, not a DIY swap. For room-by-room lamp placement, see our guide to <a href="/blog/bedroom-with-no-overhead-light-lamp-layout">lighting a bedroom with no overhead light</a>.</p>
<!--figure:bedroom-pendant-lights-cove-ceiling|Globe pendant lights beside an upholstered bed pull attention down to eye level in a calm, neutral bedroom.-->

<h2 id="tension-rods">4. Tension rods</h2>
<p>In a narrow room, such as a hallway, box room or small bedroom, tension rods are a genuinely no-drill option. Fit several across the span, a little below the ceiling and not touching it, then slide pre-hemmed curtain panels onto them or drape long lengths of cloth over them.</p>
<p>The result is a soft “false ceiling” held up entirely by pressure against the walls, with no holes and no contact with the texture. That makes it one of the lowest-risk ways to mask a ceiling you suspect may contain asbestos. Choose rods rated for the span and weight, protect the paintwork at each end with the rod’s rubber pads, and keep fabric clear of lights and alarms.</p>

<h2 id="faux-beams">5. Faux beams</h2>
<p>Lightweight polyurethane faux beams can break up a “sea of cottage cheese” and give a room a rustic or loft-like look, with the texture between them reading as background. The catch is that they must be fixed to the ceiling. In a rental, that’s only an option with your landlord’s written permission, after the ceiling has been tested, and with professional fixing. Adhesive strips are not a safe substitute.</p>
<p>Without that permission, skip them. You can create a similar sense of structure lower down with a strong rug, a large piece of art and long, low furniture that leads the eye horizontally.</p>

<h2 id="temporary-wallpaper">6. Temporary wallpaper</h2>
<p>Peel-and-stick wallpaper has come a long way, but it doesn’t belong on a popcorn ceiling: it won’t grip the bumps, and pulling it off can take texture with it. Mounting wallpapered foam boards to the ceiling with tape or glue carries the same risks, so we’d avoid that too.</p>
<p>Use temporary wallpaper where it helps without touching the ceiling: on a feature wall (with permission, after testing a hidden patch), the back of a bookcase, or a freestanding headboard panel. A bold pattern at eye level pulls attention away from the ceiling just as effectively.</p>

<h2 id="canopy-zoning">7. Canopy zoning</h2>
<p>If covering the whole ceiling feels daunting, focus on zones. In a bedroom, a canopy bed is the ultimate solution: choose a four-poster or canopy frame with a solid fabric top, or drape flame-retardant fabric over the frame, and you have a “ceiling within a ceiling”.</p>
<p>When you’re lying in bed, which is when you’re most likely to stare upwards, you’ll see fabric instead of dusty texture. The same idea works over a desk or reading chair with a freestanding canopy or a large floor-standing parasol designed for indoor use. Keep any canopy clear of lamps and heaters.</p>

<h2 id="fresh-paint">8. Fresh paint</h2>
<p>A tired, yellowed ceiling can look much better freshly painted, and a flat or ultra-matte ceiling white helps texture recede, while any sheen picks out every bump. But painting a ceiling is a landlord decision, not a renter project. Ask your landlord whether they’re willing to repaint, and leave it to them and their decorator. On an older textured ceiling that hasn’t been tested, painting isn’t advised at all, as rollers and wet paint can disturb the coating.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>The safest cover-ups are the ones that never touch the ceiling: fabric hung from the walls, tension rods, freestanding canopies and lighting that keeps the eye low. Skip anything glued, taped or painted overhead, ask your landlord about asbestos testing, and record the ceiling’s condition on move-in day. Do that, and you can enjoy a room you love and still hand back the keys with your deposit intact.</p>

<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="cover-asbestos-ceiling">Is it safe to cover a popcorn ceiling that might contain asbestos?</h3>
<p>Masking it without contact, such as fabric on tension rods or a freestanding canopy below the ceiling, is lower risk than any attempt at removal, because the coating isn’t disturbed. Anything that touches the surface, including tape, adhesive, fixings or paint, still needs the ceiling tested first and your landlord’s written permission.</p>
<h3 id="adhesive-hooks-ceiling">Will adhesive hooks hold fabric on a textured ceiling?</h3>
<p>Don’t put them on the ceiling. They grip textured surfaces poorly, and removing them can pull the texture away. Mount removable hooks on the walls just below the ceiling line instead, check the weight rating, test a hidden spot first and drape the fabric between them.</p>
<h3 id="clean-popcorn-ceiling">How do I clean a popcorn ceiling before covering it?</h3>
<p>If the ceiling may be older or hasn’t been tested, don’t vacuum, brush or dab it. Leave it alone, or ask your landlord to arrange cleaning. Covering it without contact means you don’t need to clean it first.</p>
<h3 id="lose-deposit">Will these methods cause me to lose my deposit?</h3>
<p>Methods that never touch the ceiling, such as tension rods, freestanding canopies and lamps, are the least likely to cause problems. Wall-mounted hooks or brackets can still mark paint, so get permission and test first. Photograph the room before you start and again after you take everything down, and keep the photos with your inventory.</p>
<h3 id="budget-friendly">Which method is the most budget-friendly?</h3>
<p>Rearranging lamps and swapping bulbs and shades usually costs the least. For coverage, second-hand flame-retardant curtains or fabric hung from tension rods or wall hooks is an affordable way to soften a large area quickly.</p>

